 "digest": "This week's digest focuses on financial infrastructure and autonomy in the Global South, with particular attention to digital devices and debt dynamics. New Political Economy publishes two complementary pieces: Nigeria's eNaira rollout as an infrastructural response to financial subordination, and the ECB's wholesale CBDC initiative as a strategic geopolitical technology—together illuminating how monetary devices encode and contest dependencies. ANU's analysis of Sri Lanka's sovereign debt crisis and IMF conditionality, paired with work on international financial centres' asymmetric capital flows, grounds these technical questions in the lived subordination thesis. BRICS scholarship documents the coalition's internal fractures and de-dollarization constraints, revealing the limits of South–South financial integration. India's Account Aggregator ecosystem, studied in Fintech and Digital Accounting Review, exemplifies how public digital infrastructure can reshape credit markets and financial inclusion within a Southern centre; offshore financial centres' funding networks, examined through liquidity shocks, illustrate crisis-contingent embeddedness. Excluded this week: generic ESG and Islamic finance benchmarking studies, unbanked retail CBDC adoption behaviour, and pan-African policy surveys lacking institutional grounding."
